    <!-- Slide 3: Regional Share Shift -->
    <section class="slide">
        <div class="slide-content">
            <div class="slide-header reveal">
                <div class="slide-number">02</div>
                <h2>Europe and the US Fell from 90% of Emissions in 1900 to Under One-Third Today</h2>
            </div>
            <div class="split-layout">
                <div class="split-text reveal">
                    <p>The distribution of global emissions has shifted profoundly over the last century.</p>
                    <ul class="bullet-list">
                        <li><strong>1900:</strong> Europe and the US accounted for >90% of global CO₂ emissions.</li>
                        <li><strong>1950:</strong> Still represented over 85% of the total.</li>
                        <li><strong>Today:</strong> Europe and the US account for less than 33%.</li>
                        <li><strong>The Rise of Asia:</strong> Led by China, Asia now dominates and produces roughly half of all global emissions.</li>
                    </ul>
                </div>
                <div class="split-media reveal">
                    <div class="chart-placeholder">
                        <div class="chart-placeholder-icon">📈</div>
                        <h3>Stacked Area Chart</h3>
                        <p>Showing the historical rise of Asia's share and the relative decline of Europe and North America from 1900 to 2024.</p>
                    </div>
                </div>
            </div>
        </div>
    </section>

    <!-- Slide 4: China Dominates Share -->
    <section class="slide">
        <div class="slide-content">
            <div class="slide-header reveal">
                <div class="slide-number">03</div>
                <h2>China Now Emits More Than One-Quarter of Global CO₂</h2>
            </div>
            <div class="split-layout">
                <div class="split-text reveal">
                    <p>Comparing country-level shares of global CO₂ emissions in 2024 reveals massive concentration among a few top emitters.</p>
                    <div class="stat-grid">
                        <div class="stat-card">
                            <div class="stat-value">~31%</div>
                            <div class="stat-label"><strong>China</strong><br>Rising, growth slowing</div>
